<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  app.plist — the one Info.plist key that cannot be written as a build setting.

  project.yml points INFOPLIST_FILE at this file for every configuration (Debug
  points at debug-url-scheme.plist, which holds the same key and one more).
  GENERATE_INFOPLIST_FILE stays on, and Xcode merges the INFOPLIST_KEY_ settings
  in project.yml into whatever this file holds, so the usage strings, the launch
  screen and the scene manifest come from there as before.

  The key is the list of card applets Foxy may speak to over NFC, by name. iOS
  hands the app a card only if the card answers to one of these names, so a
  bank card or a passport held to the phone is never Foxy's to talk to. There
  is one name: the applet on a Foxy card (applet/build.xml in
  https://github.com/getfoxy/card), its package's nine bytes and 01.
  Foxy/Flashcard/CardGate.swift holds the same name and checks every command
  against it, and FoxyTests holds the two equal.

  It declares no URL scheme, no document type and no queried scheme: a Release
  Foxy registers none (tests/deep-links.js).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.developer.nfc.readersession.iso7816.select-identifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>F0464F58594341524401</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Export compliance: Foxy uses only exempt encryption — HTTPS/TLS and Tor
	     (standard), and secp256k1 for signing (authentication). It does not use
	     non-exempt encryption, so it needs no self-classification report and
	     App Store Connect never asks for it on upload. -->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
